/*
🔹 Time Complexity

O(n)
Each triplet is processed once.

🔹 Space Complexity

O(1)
Only 3 booleans (constant space).

🔹 Intuition (step-by-step)

We want to form target = [a, b, c] by merging triplets.

When merging, each dimension takes the maximum value seen so far.

So:

Any triplet that exceeds the target in any dimension is useless → discard it.

Among valid triplets:

We only care if some triplet provides exactly target[i] for each dimension i.

If we can find:

one triplet matching target[0]

one triplet matching target[1]

one triplet matching target[2]

Then merging them will produce the target.
*/
class Solution {
    public boolean mergeTriplets(int[][] triplets, int[] target) {
        Set<Integer> s = new HashSet<>();
        for(int t[]:triplets){
            if(t[0] > target[0] || t[1] > target[1] || t[2] > target[2]){
                continue;
            }
            for(int i=0;i<3;i++){
                if(t[i]==target[i]){
                    s.add(i);
                }
            }
        }
        return s.size()==3;
    }
}

